DUST-POWER — Theoretical Framework v0.4

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

This version integrates the validated audit of v0.3 and corrects three structural defects: the conflation of atmospheric opacity, secular array deposition, and sampling schedule in the flux indicator; the implicit transfer of MER thermal derating to InSight cells; and the use of binned expected calibration error on a tail set that is too small to support it. The framework is a prospective protocol. No experiment, no physical test, no simulator run, and no data analysis have been performed at the time of writing. Every numerical anchor below is either a published value, an explicitly declared assumption with a sensitivity band, or a threshold to be locked after the InSight data audit and before evaluation. The contribution remains positioned downstream of existing probabilistic dust forecasting and existing integrated task planning, and is restricted to the question of whether calibrated probabilistic reserve information changes de-loading decisions in a simulator calibrated on public InSight production observations.

---

1. Status of this document

This document specifies a study that has not yet been executed. It contains:

· formal definitions and a decomposition of the observable flux indicator (§4);
· a first-order energy model with a declared thermal envelope (§5);
· hypotheses with pre-specified targets (§6);
· a three-stage value-of-information gate (§6.4);
· metrics based on consistent, optimally binned reliability estimation (§7);
· a refutation protocol with pre-registered thresholds (§9);
· a data manifest specification and a work order (§10).

No result, no calibration figure, and no decision comparison is reported. Any statement of the form “the model achieves” is absent by construction. All thresholds are written as \square placeholders that must be instantiated and timestamped after the audit of §10 and before the evaluation of §9, in a pre-registration record.

