4. Decomposed flux model

4.1 Time scales and filtration

Let s\in\{0,\dots,S\} index sols and k\in\{1,\dots,K_s\} index samples within sol s. Nominal cadence is 30\ \mathrm{s} until sol 426 and 4\ \mathrm{s} thereafter, with documented gaps. \mathcal{F}_s is the filtration generated by observations available at the end of sol s. All policies must be \mathcal{F}_s-measurable.

4.2 Noon-peak geometry-normalised indicator

For the two hardwired arrays, define the diurnal mask m(s,k)=1 when the sample is present and \mu(s,k)=\cos z(s,k)>\mu_{\min}>0. Let

k_{\mathrm{noon}}(s)=\arg\max_{k:\,m(s,k)=1}\ \mu(s,k),

subject to a declared noon window |\,\ell(s,k_{\mathrm{noon}})-\ell_{\mathrm{noon}}\,|\le \Delta\ell, where \ell is local solar hour. The noon-peak indicator is

\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}(s)=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{j=1}^{2}\frac{i_j\!\left(s,k_{\mathrm{noon}}(s)\right)}{i_j^{\mathrm{ref}}}\,\mathbf{1}\{m(s,k_{\mathrm{noon}})=1\},

with i_j^{\mathrm{ref}}>0 a normalisation reference declared on a documented illuminated window. Sols for which no sample falls in the noon window are marked unavailable; they are counted, never imputed silently. The availability fraction

\rho_{\mathrm{noon}}=\frac{1}{S+1}\sum_{s=0}^{S}\mathbf{1}\{m(s,k_{\mathrm{noon}}(s))=1\}

is reported alongside every series.

4.3 Multiplicative decomposition

Short-circuit current is approximately proportional to incident flux, so a geometry-normalised noon current is modelled as

\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}(s)=c\,G(s)\,T\!\left(\tau(s)\right)D(s)\,\varepsilon(s),

where

G(s)=\left(\frac{\bar R}{R(s)}\right)^{2}

is the heliocentric correction, T(\tau)=\exp\!\left(-\tau/\mu_{\mathrm{noon}}\right) is a direct-beam surrogate for atmospheric transmission, D(s)\in(0,1] is the secular array deposition factor, and \varepsilon(s) collects measurement noise and unmodelled effects.

Deposition model. D(s) is specified as monotone non-increasing with jumps:

D(s)=D(s-1)\left(1-\lambda(s)\right)\left(1-J(s)\right),
\qquad
\lambda(s)\in[\lambda_{\min},\lambda_{\max}],

with \lambda_{\min}=0.05\,\%/\mathrm{sol}, \lambda_{\max}=2\,\%/\mathrm{sol}, and a central value \lambda_{0}=0.2\,\%/\mathrm{sol}. The InSight-specific median reported in the array-current literature is 0.28\,\%/\mathrm{sol}, and is retained as an InSight-specific central alternative. The cleaning term J(s)\in[0,1) is non-zero only on declared cleaning events. The pair (\lambda_{0},J) is treated as a nuisance parameter vector, not as a known quantity, and every predictability result is reported across its declared range.

Detrended target variable. Define

r(s)=\log\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}(s)-\log G(s)-\log \hat D(s),

so that r(s) estimates \log\!\left(c\,T(\tau(s))\right) plus noise. All predictability targets of §6 are defined on r(s), not on \varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}(s). A conclusion whose sign reverses when \hat D\equiv 1 is reported as confounded (criterion K10).

4.4 Two disjoint production approaches

Observational approach (O). No dust factor is applied to measured currents; the measured current already contains the attenuation.

P_{\mathrm{pv}}^{\mathrm{(O)}}(s)=\alpha\,\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}(s)\,\frac{\mu_{\mathrm{noon}}(s)}{\mu_{\mathrm{ref}}}\,V_{\mathrm{op}}(s),

restricted to the co-observed subset where E-0789 is available at a cadence compatible with the array currents. If that subset is proper, the restriction is reported as a selection effect, together with a comparison of \varphi_{\mathrm{noon}} distributions on included versus excluded sols.

Physical approach (P). Reserved for scenario generation and transfer testing:

P_{\mathrm{pv}}^{\mathrm{(P)}}(s)=\eta_{\mathrm{pv}}A\,F_\downarrow(s)\,\mathrm{DF}(s),
\qquad
F_\downarrow(s)=F_{0}\,G(s)\exp\!\left(-\frac{\tau(s)}{\mu(s)}\right).

Approach P is a direct-beam model. At high opacity, diffuse sky light dominates the array input; the 2007 MER storms blocked approximately 99 % of direct sunlight while the arrays continued to deliver power from diffuse illumination. Approach P therefore underestimates production precisely in the tail, and is inadmissible as a tail validator. It is admissible only as a generator of moderate-opacity scenarios and as the forcing model of the transfer test of §8.

Non-mixing rule. O and P may be compared but never combined without explicit justification. Additionally, a circularity check is mandatory: if \tau used in P is retrieved from the same currents that define \varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}, P is invalid for empirical evaluation. The check is implemented as a declared correlation test between \varphi_{\mathrm{noon}} and any candidate \tau series on the sols where P is proposed; a high correlation is declared circular and P is confined to the simulator.

