5. First-order energy model with a declared thermal envelope

5.1 Storage

With E(s)\in[0,E_{\max}] in \mathrm{W\cdot h} and E_{\max}=C_{\mathrm{nom}}V_{\mathrm{nom}}, where C_{\mathrm{nom}} is the InSight nameplate capacity of 25\ \mathrm{A\cdot h} and V_{\mathrm{nom}} is a declared nominal bus voltage,

P_{\mathrm{net}}(s)=P_{\mathrm{pv}}(s)-P_{\mathrm{load}}(s),

E(s+1)=\operatorname{clip}\!\left(
E(s)+\eta_{c}\kappa_{T}\,[P_{\mathrm{net}}(s)]^{+}\Delta t_s
-\frac{[-P_{\mathrm{net}}(s)]^{+}}{\eta_{d}\kappa_{T}}\Delta t_s,\;
0,\;E_{\max}\right),

with [x]^{+}=\max(x,0) and \eta_c,\eta_d\in(0,1]. The subtraction is explicit: in discharge P_{\mathrm{net}}<0, the first increment vanishes and the second is positive.

5.2 Thermal envelope as a declared assumption

InSight cells are rated for charge and discharge from approximately -30^\circ\mathrm{C} to +35^\circ\mathrm{C} and are housed in a warm box actively maintained above a low-temperature floor. No public cell-level capacity-versus-temperature curve at fine resolution is assumed available for this study. The derating factor is therefore declared as an assumption:

\kappa_{T}\in[\kappa_{T}^{\min},1],
\qquad
\kappa_{T}^{\min}=\square,

with \kappa_{T}^{\min} fixed after the audit of §10 from mission documentation, and with the sensitivity band reported explicitly. MER-derived derating values are not transferred, because the cell chemistry and the thermal architecture differ.

5.3 Loads coupled to the environment

P_{\mathrm{load}}(s)=P_{\mathrm{crit}}(s)+u(s)P_{\mathrm{non}}(s),
\qquad u(s)\in\{0,1\},

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(s)=P_{\mathrm{avionics}}+P_{\mathrm{heater}}\!\left(\tau(s),T_{\mathrm{env}}(s)\right).

Heater demand is coupled to the forecast variable: dust storms suppress the diurnal temperature swing and warm the environment, so P_{\mathrm{heater}} decreases as \tau increases. This coupling breaks a paired-scenario design that holds P_{\mathrm{crit}} fixed. Pairing is therefore defined over the environmental realisation \omega=(\tau(\cdot),T_{\mathrm{env}}(\cdot),G(\cdot)), not over a constant critical load: two policies are compared on the same \omega, so the coupling is preserved identically on both sides.

5.4 Failure events

Reserve event.

E_{\mathrm{crit}}=(1-\mathrm{DoD}_{\max})E_{\max},
\qquad
\mathcal{R}_H(s)=\left\{\min_{1\le j\le H}E(s+j)<E_{\mathrm{crit}}\right\}.

Service deficit, two components. The sol-level power test alone hides the nightly depth of discharge, which is the binding constraint. Define

\mathcal{D}^{P}_H(s)=\left\{\exists j\in[1,H]:P_{\mathrm{pv}}(s+j)+P_{\mathrm{bat}}^{\max}<P_{\mathrm{crit}}(s+j)\right\},

\mathcal{D}^{E}_H(s)=\left\{\exists j\in[1,H]:\ \mathrm{DoD}(j)>\mathrm{DoD}_{\max}\right\},
\qquad
\mathcal{D}_H(s)=\mathcal{D}^{P}_H(s)\cup\mathcal{D}^{E}_H(s),

where \mathrm{DoD}(j) is computed on the intra-sol trajectory, not on the sol-mean state, so that the nightly excursion is resolved. P_{\mathrm{bat}}^{\max} is retained only inside \mathcal{D}^{P} as a declared power-limit assumption; it does not replace the energy-based test.

Ex post evaluation. \mathcal{D}_H(s) is evaluated in the simulator on the realised production and the realised storage trajectory. The policy \pi uses a forecast; the failure event uses the realisation. The probability of interest,

p_H(s;\pi,\theta)=\mathbb{P}\!\left(\mathcal{D}_H(s)\mid\mathcal{F}_s,\pi,\theta\right),

is a simulator-conditional frequency, not a measured failure rate on InSight.

5.5 Decision problem

\pi^{\star}=\arg\min_{\pi\in\Pi}\ \mathbb{E}\!\left[\sum_{s}c_{1}\left(1-u(s)\right)P_{\mathrm{non}}(s)\Delta t_{s}+c_{2}\mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{D}_H}\right]

subject to

\mathbb{P}\!\left(\mathcal{D}_H\mid\pi\right)\le\alpha .

No optimal policy exists without a declared triple (c_{1},c_{2},\alpha); results are a family of compromises.

5.6 Reference policy anchored to the observed operational trigger

The reference is not an arbitrary threshold. InSight entered safe mode on 7 May 2022 after a sustained energy shortfall, having fallen to roughly 500\ \mathrm{W\cdot h} per sol. The reference policy is therefore defined as an energy-threshold safe-mode rule:

\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}:\quad u(s)=0\ \Longleftrightarrow\ \hat E(s+H_{\mathrm{safe}})<E_{\mathrm{safe}},

with E_{\mathrm{safe}} calibrated so that, when replayed on the 2022 environmental realisation, the trigger fires at the observed date. H_{\mathrm{safe}} is a declared look-ahead, not an oracle. This constitutes an E1-anchored retrodiction target for \pi_{\mathrm{ref}} and is reported as such; it is not used to validate any forecast model.

