6. Hypotheses

6.1 H1a — Dust-driven transmission deficit is predictable (E1/E2)

Y_{s,H}=\mathbf{1}\!\left[\min_{1\le j\le H}r(s+j)<q\right],

with r(s) the detrended variable of §4.3 and q a low-transmission threshold fixed after the audit. Windows with excessive missingness are excluded by a pre-declared rule and are never labelled “no deficit.”

H1a: a probabilistic predictor conditioned on r(s), its recent trend, and the declared availability pattern achieves a Brier skill score against a persistence reference of at least \delta_{\mathrm{B}} on held-out episodes.

6.2 H1b — Reserve state is predictable under a frozen naive policy (E3)

Z_{s,H}=\mathbf{1}\!\left[\hat E(s+H)\ \text{under}\ \pi_{\mathrm{naive}}\ \text{falls below}\ E_{\mathrm{crit}}\right],

with \pi_{\mathrm{naive}} frozen before evaluation as u(s)=1 if \hat E(s)>0.3E_{\max} and u(s)=0 otherwise, and with the initial state sampled from a declared distribution E(0)\sim\mathcal{U}[0.4,0.8]\,E_{\max}, not from an observed state, since no ground truth on E(s) exists.

H1b separates “the transmission is hard to forecast” from “the energy dynamics amplify or damp flux errors.”

6.3 Confound criterion

Because r(s) depends on the assumed deposition model, both H1a and H1b are reported under \hat D from the declared band and under \hat D\equiv 1. A sign reversal is reported as confounded (K10) and no forecast claim is made.

6.4 Oracle-gated value of information

Let \pi_{\mathrm{oracle}} be a perfect-foresight policy on the same scenario set. The gate proceeds in three stages.

Stage 1 — structural gate. Compute

\Delta_{\mathrm{oracle}}=\mathbb{E}\!\left[\mathcal{C}(\pi_{\mathrm{ref}})\right]-\mathbb{E}\!\left[\mathcal{C}(\pi_{\mathrm{oracle}})\right],

where \mathcal{C} is the declared cost functional. If \Delta_{\mathrm{oracle}} is below a pre-declared floor \Delta_{\min}, the decision problem is declared forecast-irrelevant and H2 is not tested. No forecast, however well calibrated, can improve on a reference that is already near-optimal.

Stage 2 — conditional VoI. Restricted to states where the forecast-based and reference actions disagree,

\mathrm{VoI}=\mathbb{E}\!\left[\mathcal{C}\mid\pi_{\mathrm{ref}},\mathcal{A}=1\right]-\mathbb{E}\!\left[\mathcal{C}\mid\pi_{\mathrm{DP}},\mathcal{A}=1\right],

with \mathcal{A}(s) the disagreement indicator. \mathrm{VoI}\le 0 on the disagreement set is a structural finding, not a calibration failure.

Stage 3 — decision comparison. Only if stages 1 and 2 pass is H2 tested.

6.5 H2 and robustness clause

\mathrm{Sac}(\pi)=\sum_{s}\left(1-u_{\pi}(s)\right)P_{\mathrm{non}}(s)\Delta t_{s}.

H2: at \mathrm{Sac}(\pi_{\mathrm{DP}})\le\mathrm{Sac}(\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}), the expected critical deficit under \pi_{\mathrm{DP}} is lower than under \pi_{\mathrm{ref}} on paired environmental realisations.

H2-R. \pi_{\mathrm{DP}} must not increase the 95th-percentile critical deficit relative to \pi_{\mathrm{ref}} at matched sacrifice. A policy better on average but worse in the worst cases is inadmissible.

6.6 H3 — Failure structure

With pre-specified severity classes \tau_{\mathrm{low}},\tau_{\mathrm{mid}},\tau_{\mathrm{high}} and missingness classes \rho_{\mathrm{low}},\rho_{\mathrm{mid}}, the reliability component of the Brier decomposition is monotone non-decreasing in both, across independent episodes. Non-monotonicity rejects H3 and is reported as information about the failure structure, not as a failure of the programme.

