7. Metrics

7.1 Consistent reliability estimation

Binned expected calibration error is not used. Reliability is estimated with the CORP approach (consistent, optimally binned reliability diagrams by pool-adjacent-violators isotonic regression), which is provably consistent, bin-free, and reproducible, and which yields a generalised Brier decomposition

\mathrm{BS}=\mathrm{Rel}-\mathrm{Res}+\mathrm{Unc},

where \mathrm{Rel} is the reliability component, \mathrm{Res} the resolution, and \mathrm{Unc} the uncertainty. All three are reported, so that a low Brier score driven by high resolution and poor reliability is not mistaken for calibration. Discrimination (AUC) is reported separately.

7.2 Tail criterion

Let \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{tail}} be the set of episodes whose maximum optical depth exceeds a physical threshold \tau_{\mathrm{high}}, fixed at the maximum reported during the late InSight mission rather than at a statistical percentile, because a percentile of a short record is not operationally meaningful. The tail criterion is defined on the CORP reliability component:

\Delta_{\mathrm{tail}}^{\mathrm{CORP}}=\mathrm{Rel}\!\left(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{tail}}\right)-\mathrm{Rel}\!\left(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{bulk}}\right).

If the number of independent tail episodes is below a pre-declared minimum N_{\mathrm{tail}}^{\min}, the tail criterion is reported as inconclusive, never as passed.

