8. Transfer test for the tail

Because no global dust event occurred during the InSight mission and no independent high-cadence retrieval is archived, the tail is not empirically testable on InSight. It is tested by transfer instead.

The 2018 Opportunity collapse provides an E2 benchmark: published power series show a fall from approximately 645\ \mathrm{W\cdot h} on 1 June 2018 to approximately 22\ \mathrm{W\cdot h} on 10 June 2018, a collapse over about nine sols. This fixes the decision-relevant horizon at approximately five to ten sols, independently of any assumption made here.

The simulator is forced with an Opportunity-equivalent \tau trajectory and must reproduce a collapse of the published magnitude and duration within a declared tolerance. Failure indicates that the radiative transfer treatment — specifically the absence of diffuse illumination — is inadequate, and the tail is declared not reproducible (K5). The transfer test is a falsification test of the simulator's tail physics; it is not a validation of the InSight calibration.

