11. Declared epistemic limits

1. No experiment, no physical test, no simulator run has been performed. This document is a protocol. Every threshold is a placeholder; every number is either published or a declared assumption.
2. One usable mission. MER engineering telemetry is legally restricted and cannot serve as an empirical validation base; InSight is the only public source. The Opportunity 2018 series is used as an E2 transfer benchmark, not as validation data.
3. No ground truth on E(s). The storage state is E3 throughout. Validation bears on production (E1/E2), never on real storage management.
4. No direct power measurement. \varphi_{\mathrm{noon}} is a flux proxy; the bus voltage is E1 at the bus and E2 at the maximum-power point.
5. First-order storage model. Temperature derating is a declared band, not a model; aging, low-temperature charge limits, voltage sag, and auxiliary losses are omitted by declaration.
6. \tau is E3. No independent retrieval is assumed. Approach P is confined to scenario generation and transfer testing, and is inadmissible as a tail validator because it omits diffuse illumination.
7. The tail is not empirically testable on InSight. No global event occurred during the mission. The tail claim is conditional on the transfer test of §8 and is otherwise reported as inconclusive.
8. Cost-function dependence. Results are a family of compromises over (c_{1},c_{2},\alpha), not a unique optimum.
9. Probabilistic dust forecasting is already covered by the concurrent ESS Open Archive preprint of July 2026, and integrated energy-aware task planning is already covered by the WCCM-ECCOMAS 2026 framework. DUST-POWER claims neither. Its question is narrower: whether calibrated probabilistic reserve information, with explicit missing-data and deposition representation, changes de-loading decisions relative to an operationally anchored reference in a simulator calibrated on public InSight production observations.

---

Verdict of v0.4

The inference chain is unchanged — observed current (E1) → decomposed and detrended flux (E2) → simulated state (E3) → conditional decision (E3) — but three of its links are now guarded. The flux indicator no longer confounds opacity with secular deposition and sampling schedule, and the confound criterion K10 makes the residual dependence explicit. The thermal envelope is anchored to the InSight cell qualification and to an operational thermal architecture rather than to MER data, and the fragility criterion K9 prevents a declared band from being presented as a modelled capacity. The tail criterion is moved from a difference of unstable binned errors to a CORP reliability gap with episode-level bootstrap and an explicit “inconclusive” outcome when the tail set is too small.

The two gates added in this version are the ones most likely to determine whether the study is worth executing at all. The oracle gate asks, before any tuning, whether the decision problem admits improvement at all; if a perfect-foresight policy barely improves on an operationally anchored reference, the forecast cannot help and the honest conclusion is structural irrelevance. The transfer test asks, before any tail claim, whether the simulator can reproduce a documented collapse; if it cannot, the tail is not reproducible and the claim is restricted to the bulk.

The next deliverable remains the InSight data manifest, now extended to the 2022 storm window and now requiring file-level verification of the E-0789 cadence, because the credibility of the observational approach depends on whether the bus voltage supports sol-level power inference or only co-observed-subset inference, and because the only operationally anchored reference policy available in the public record is the 2022 safe-mode trigger.

