Appendix B — Storage dynamics and failure events

B.1 Monotone recursion

Proposition B.1. Under the update

E(s+1)=\operatorname{clip}\!\left(E(s)+\eta_c\kappa_T[P_{\mathrm{net}}(s)]^{+}\Delta t_s-\frac{[-P_{\mathrm{net}}(s)]^{+}}{\eta_d\kappa_T}\Delta t_s,\;0,\;E_{\max}\right),

with \eta_c,\eta_d\in(0,1], \kappa_T\in(0,1], \Delta t_s>0, and clipping interior (i.e., no active constraint):

(i) P_{\mathrm{net}}(s)>0\Rightarrow E(s+1)>E(s);
(ii) P_{\mathrm{net}}(s)<0\Rightarrow E(s+1)<E(s);
(iii) P_{\mathrm{net}}(s)=0\Rightarrow E(s+1)=E(s);
(iv) E(0)\in[0,E_{\max}]\Rightarrow E(s)\in[0,E_{\max}] for all s.

Proof. Let \Delta E=\eta_c\kappa_T[P_{\mathrm{net}}]^{+}\Delta t_s-[-P_{\mathrm{net}}]^{+}/(\eta_d\kappa_T)\Delta t_s.

(i) If P_{\mathrm{net}}>0, then [P_{\mathrm{net}}]^{+}=P_{\mathrm{net}}>0 and [-P_{\mathrm{net}}]^{+}=0, so \Delta E=\eta_c\kappa_TP_{\mathrm{net}}\Delta t_s>0, hence E(s+1)=E(s)+\Delta E>E(s) under interior clipping.

(ii) If P_{\mathrm{net}}<0, then [P_{\mathrm{net}}]^{+}=0 and [-P_{\mathrm{net}}]^{+}=-P_{\mathrm{net}}>0, so \Delta E=P_{\mathrm{net}}\Delta t_s/(\eta_d\kappa_T)<0, hence E(s+1)<E(s).

(iii) Immediate.

(iv) The map x\mapsto\operatorname{clip}(x,0,E_{\max}) projects onto [0,E_{\max}]. By induction, E(0)\in[0,E_{\max}] and each update is followed by a projection, so E(s)\in[0,E_{\max}] for all s. \square

B.2 Non-nesting of \mathcal{D}^{P}_H and \mathcal{D}^{E}_H

Proposition B.2. \mathcal{D}^{P}_H and \mathcal{D}^{E}_H are not nested: there exist scenarios with \mathcal{D}^{P}_H=\varnothing and \mathcal{D}^{E}_H\neq\varnothing, and scenarios with \mathcal{D}^{P}_H\neq\varnothing and \mathcal{D}^{E}_H=\varnothing.

Proof. (i) Power-sufficient, energy-violating. Take P_{\mathrm{pv}}(s)=200 W for 10 h of daylight, P_{\mathrm{load}}=50 W for 24 h, P_{\mathrm{bat}}^{\max}=300 W, E_{\max}=500 Wh, E(0)=400 Wh, \mathrm{DoD}_{\max}=0.5, \eta_c=\eta_d=1. Net solar over the sol: 2000-1200=+800 Wh, clipped at E_{\max}. Overnight the battery must supply 50\times 14=700 Wh from E=500 Wh, so the DoD reaches 700/500=1.4>0.5=\mathrm{DoD}_{\max}, and \mathcal{D}^{E}_H\neq\varnothing. Yet P_{\mathrm{load}}=50<P_{\mathrm{bat}}^{\max}=300 at every instant, so \mathcal{D}^{P}_H=\varnothing.

(ii) Power-violating, energy-safe. Take P_{\mathrm{pv}}(s)=0, P_{\mathrm{load}}(s)=200 W for one hour, P_{\mathrm{bat}}^{\max}=100 W, E(s)=400 Wh, E_{\max}=500 Wh, \mathrm{DoD}_{\max}=0.95. Then P_{\mathrm{pv}}+P_{\mathrm{bat}}^{\max}=100<200=P_{\mathrm{crit}}, so \mathcal{D}^{P}_H\neq\varnothing. The battery would have to deliver 200 Wh in one hour, an average of 200 W >P_{\mathrm{bat}}^{\max}, but the energy constraint is not violated if the load can be curtailed to 100 W for two hours; the DoD stays at 200/500=0.4<0.95. Hence \mathcal{D}^{E}_H=\varnothing.

The two events therefore overlap without nesting; the union \mathcal{D}_H=\mathcal{D}^{P}_H\cup\mathcal{D}^{E}_H is strictly more conservative than either. \square

B.3 Why the sol-mean state is insufficient

Proposition B.3. There exist scenarios in which the sol-mean state satisfies \bar E(s)>E_{\mathrm{crit}} and the sol-mean power satisfies P_{\mathrm{pv}}>P_{\mathrm{load}}, yet the intra-sol trajectory violates the DoD constraint.

Proof. Consider a sol with P_{\mathrm{pv}}(s)=300 W for 8 h, P_{\mathrm{load}}=100 W for 24 h, E_{\max}=400 Wh, E(0)=200 Wh, P_{\mathrm{bat}}^{\max}=100 W. During daylight, P_{\mathrm{net}}=+200 W, so E climbs toward E_{\max}; over 8 h it would add 1600 Wh, clipped at 400 Wh. During night (16 h), P_{\mathrm{net}}=-100 W, so the required draw is 1600 Wh, far exceeding E_{\max}, and the trajectory E(t) hits zero within 4 h of sunset, then saturates at zero. The sol-mean energy \bar E(s) computed as a time-average is below E_{\max}/2 in the second half of the sol, but the sol-mean power balance is P_{\mathrm{pv}}>P_{\mathrm{load}} because the daytime surplus dominates. A test based on sol-mean state misses the night-time excursion entirely; the DoD is computed on the trajectory, not the mean. \square

