Appendix C — Flux decomposition and detrending

C.1 Multiplicative model

Proposition C.1. Under the physical assumptions that (i) short-circuit current is approximately proportional to incident flux, (ii) the heliocentric correction is G(s)=(\bar R/R(s))^2, (iii) atmospheric attenuation is exponential in airmass, and (iv) deposition acts multiplicatively on the array transmission, the noon-peak indicator satisfies

\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}(s)=c\,G(s)\,T(\tau(s))\,D(s)\,\varepsilon(s),

with c>0, T(\tau)=\exp(-\tau/\mu_{\mathrm{noon}}), D(s)\in(0,1], and \varepsilon(s) a noise factor with \mathbb{E}[\log\varepsilon]=0 and finite variance.

Proof. Direct substitution of (i)–(iv). The constant c absorbs the normalisation reference i_j^{\mathrm{ref}} and geometric factors. \square

C.2 Scale invariance of the flux proxy

Proposition C.2. Suppose \varphi_{\mathrm{noon}} is rescaled to \varphi'_{\mathrm{noon}}=c'\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}} with c'>0. If the detrended target is correspondingly rescaled (r'(s)=r(s)+\log c') and the threshold is shifted q'=q+\log c', then p_H, the Brier score, the reliability components, and the optimal decision u^{\star}(s) are unchanged.

Proof. P(\min_{j}r(s+j)<q)=P(\min_j r'(s+j)<q') by monotone transformation. The Brier score depends only on predicted probabilities and outcomes; since predicted probabilities are unchanged, the Brier score is unchanged. Since the cost functional depends only on (u,\mathcal{D}_H) and \mathcal{D}_H depends only on the probabilities, the optimal decision is unchanged. \square

C.3 Detrending identifiability

Proposition C.3. Assume (i) \varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}=cGTD\varepsilon with D monotone non-increasing except at a set of cleaning sols \mathcal{J}=\{s_1<\dots<s_m\}; (ii) on clear-sky sols, \tau is below a threshold so that T\approx 1; (iii) \varepsilon is mean-one multiplicative noise. Then the pair (\log c+\log D,\;\{\log(1-J(s_i))\}_{i=1}^m) is identifiable up to a single additive constant on the log scale.

Proof. On clear-sky sols, \log\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}-\log G=\log c+\log D+\log\varepsilon. The drift between consecutive clear-sky sols not separated by a cleaning event yields \log D(s+1)-\log D(s)=\log(1-\lambda(s))\approx -\lambda(s) after averaging over \varepsilon. Across a cleaning event s_i, \log D(s_i^{+})-\log D(s_i^{-})=\log(1-J(s_i)). The absolute level of \log c+\log D requires one anchor: a known \tau or a known c or the boundary condition D(0)=1. After anchoring, the entire trajectory is identified. The ambiguity is a single additive constant, which is absorbed into c. \square

C.4 Confounding without detrending

Proposition C.4. Suppose the researcher defines r(s)=\log\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}-\log G-\log\hat D(s) with \hat D\neq D. Then

r(s)=\log c+\log T(\tau(s))+\log\frac{D(s)}{\hat D(s)}+\log\varepsilon(s).

If D is strictly monotone non-increasing and \hat D\equiv 1, then \mathbb{E}[r(s)] is strictly decreasing in s on sols with constant \tau. Consequently, any persistence test on r(s) has positive skill purely from the drift term.

Proof. Trivial substitution. The strict monotonicity of D implies that \log D is strictly decreasing between cleaning events; a persistence predictor inherits this decrease, so its Brier skill against persistence is not evidence about \tau-predictability. \square

Corollary C.4.1 (K10). If a conclusion reverses sign when \hat D\equiv 1 is replaced by the anchored \hat D from Proposition C.3, the result is confounded by deposition and is reported as such.

C.5 Sampling-selection bias in the co-observed subset

Proposition C.5. Let A(s)\in\{0,1\} indicate co-observation of both E-0789 and the array currents. Assume (i) \mathbb{P}(A=1\mid\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}) is non-decreasing in \varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}; (ii) 0<\mathbb{P}(A=1)<1. Then

\mathbb{E}[\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}\mid A=1]\ge\mathbb{E}[\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}],

with equality iff A\perp\!\!\!\perp\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}.

Proof. By Bayes,

\mathbb{E}[\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}\mid A=1]=\frac{\mathbb{E}[\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}\,\mathbb{P}(A=1\mid\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}})]}{\mathbb{P}(A=1)}.

Since \mathbb{P}(A=1\mid\cdot) is non-decreasing and \varphi_{\mathrm{noon}} is comonotone with itself, Chebyshev's other inequality (the covariance inequality for comonotone functions) gives

\mathbb{E}[\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}\,\mathbb{P}(A=1\mid\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}})]\ge \mathbb{E}[\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}]\,\mathbb{E}[\mathbb{P}(A=1\mid\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}})]=\mathbb{E}[\varphi_{\mathrm{noon}}]\,\mathbb{P}(A=1).

Dividing by \mathbb{P}(A=1) yields the claim. Equality holds iff \mathbb{P}(A=1\mid\cdot) is constant. \square

Interpretation. The co-observed subset is biased toward high-flux sols. This is the wrong direction for tail safety: the subset overrepresents mild conditions and underrepresents the very regime that the tail criterion aims to evaluate.

