Appendix E — Information structure

E.1 Measurability and causal policy

Proposition E.1. A policy \pi satisfies \pi(\mathcal{F}_s)\mapsto u(s) iff u(s) is a deterministic function of the information available at the end of sol s, and in that case u(s) is independent of \{\tau(s'),i_j(s',\cdot):s'>s\} conditionally on \mathcal{F}_s.

Proof. By definition of \mathcal{F}_s-measurability, u(s)=\pi(\mathcal{F}_s) is a function of the \sigma-algebra generated by observations up to s. Any future variable is measurable with respect to \mathcal{F}_{s'} for s'>s, which is independent of u(s) given \mathcal{F}_s by construction of the filtration. \square

E.2 Non-leakage

Proposition E.2. A policy is non-leaking iff, for all s, u(s)\perp\!\!\!\perp\{\tau(s'),i_j(s',\cdot):s'>s\}\mid\mathcal{F}_s.

Proof. Immediate from E.1. The condition is the definition of adaptedness. \square

Implementation. Splitting is by episode, not by sol; the tuning set, validation set, and test set are disjoint in episodes. Any sol-level dependence is reported as a leak.

E.3 Detrending is \mathcal{F}_s-measurable

Proposition E.3. If \hat D(s) is estimated from observations up to sol s (causal estimation), then \hat D(s) is \mathcal{F}_s-measurable and the detrended target r(s) is \mathcal{F}_s-measurable. If \hat D is estimated using all sols, the target leaks future information.

Proof. Direct from the definition of the filtration. \square

Implementation. The deposition model is fitted on a training split only, and applied out-of-sample; the parameters (\lambda,J) are frozen before evaluation.

