Appendix F — Value of information

F.1 Oracle gate lemma

Lemma F.1. Let \pi^{\star}=\arg\min_{\pi\in\Pi}\mathcal{C}(\pi) be the perfect-foresight oracle, and let \Delta_{\mathrm{oracle}}=\mathcal{C}(\pi_{\mathrm{ref}})-\mathcal{C}(\pi^{\star}). For any policy \pi\in\Pi,

\mathcal{C}(\pi)\ge\mathcal{C}(\pi_{\mathrm{ref}})-\Delta_{\mathrm{oracle}}.

Proof. By the definition of the infimum, \mathcal{C}(\pi)\ge\mathcal{C}(\pi^{\star})=\mathcal{C}(\pi_{\mathrm{ref}})-\Delta_{\mathrm{oracle}}. \square

Corollary F.1.1. If \Delta_{\mathrm{oracle}}\le\Delta_{\min}, then every policy is within \Delta_{\min} of the reference, and no forecast can improve by more than \Delta_{\min}.

F.2 Conditional VoI identity

Lemma F.2. Let \mathcal{A}(s)=\mathbf{1}\{u_{\pi_{\mathrm{DP}}}(s)\neq u_{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}(s)\} and \mathrm{VoI}=\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{C}\mid\pi_{\mathrm{ref}},\mathcal{A}=1]-\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{C}\mid\pi_{\mathrm{DP}},\mathcal{A}=1]. Then

\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{C}\mid\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}]-\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{C}\mid\pi_{\mathrm{DP}}]=\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{A}=1)\cdot\mathrm{VoI}.

Proof. Decompose both conditional expectations over \{\mathcal{A}=0\} and \{\mathcal{A}=1\}. On \{\mathcal{A}=0\}, the two policies take identical actions, so their conditional expectations are equal. Hence the difference is concentrated on \{\mathcal{A}=1\} and equals \mathbb{P}(\mathcal{A}=1)\cdot\mathrm{VoI}. \square

F.3 Sharpness relative to a disagreement-frequency floor

Corollary F.3.1. A disagreement-frequency floor of the form \mathbb{P}(\mathcal{A}=1)\ge p_{\min} is necessary but not sufficient for a forecast to be useful: the improvement equals p_{\min}\cdot\mathrm{VoI}, and \mathrm{VoI} is itself bounded by \Delta_{\mathrm{oracle}}/\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{A}=1). The oracle gate is therefore sharper than any disagreement-frequency floor.

Proof. Immediate from F.1 and F.2. \square

