Appendix G — Reliability estimation

G.1 Murphy decomposition

Proposition G.1. For finite bins \{S_b\}_{b=1}^B with bin probability p_b=n_b/N and bin outcome frequency o_b, the Brier score decomposes as

\mathrm{BS}=\mathrm{Rel}-\mathrm{Res}+\mathrm{Unc},
\qquad
\mathrm{Rel}=\sum_{b=1}^B\frac{n_b}{N}(p_b-o_b)^2,
\quad
\mathrm{Res}=\sum_{b=1}^B\frac{n_b}{N}(o_b-\bar o)^2,
\quad
\mathrm{Unc}=\bar o(1-\bar o).

Proof. Write (p_i-o_i)^2=(p_i-p_b+p_b-o_i)^2=(p_i-p_b)^2+2(p_i-p_b)(p_b-o_i)+(p_b-o_i)^2. Summing over i\in S_b, the cross term vanishes because \sum_{i\in S_b}(p_i-p_b)=0. Next, (p_b-o_i)^2=(p_b-o_b+o_b-o_i)^2, and the cross term vanishes because \sum_{i\in S_b}(o_i-o_b)=0. Summing over b and dividing by N gives the result. \square

G.2 CORP consistency

Proposition G.2 (Dimitriadis, Gneiting, and Jordan 2021). The CORP estimator of the reliability diagram, defined by pool-adjacent-violators isotonic regression of the empirical bin frequencies, is pointwise consistent for the true reliability diagram under mild regularity conditions. The plug-in estimator of \mathrm{Rel} obtained from the CORP diagram is consistent for the true \mathrm{Rel}.

Proof sketch. The empirical distribution of predicted probabilities converges weakly to the true distribution. The isotonic projection of a consistent estimator onto the class of monotone functions is consistent in the sup-norm by the continuous-mapping property of isotonic regression on the monotone class. The reliability component is a continuous functional of the (calibration map, marginal distribution) pair, so the continuous-mapping theorem gives consistency. \square

G.3 Episode-level bootstrap

Proposition G.3. Suppose within-episode observations are \alpha-mixing with mixing coefficients decaying faster than any polynomial, and episodes are independent. Then a block bootstrap with block length equal to the episode length is consistent for the distribution of the reliability and resolution components; a sol-level i.i.d. bootstrap undercovers.

Proof sketch. Standard block bootstrap theory. The block bootstrap with block length \ell_n\to\infty, \ell_n=o(n), reproduces the dependence structure; under \alpha-mixing, the empirical process indexed by blocks converges weakly to the appropriate Gaussian process, so the bootstrap distribution is consistent. Sol-level i.i.d. resampling ignores within-episode dependence and underestimates the variance, hence undercovers. \square

