Appendix I — Thermal envelope

I.1 Fragility

Proposition I.1. Let u^{\star}(s;\kappa_T) be the optimal decision under the cost functional \mathcal{C}, as a function of the thermal factor. If there exists a scenario \omega and a pair \kappa_T^{\min},\kappa_T^{\max}\in(0,1] such that u^{\star}(s;\kappa_T^{\min})\neq u^{\star}(s;\kappa_T^{\max}) for some s, then no recommendation that depends on u^{\star} is admissible without a joint report of the two trajectories.

Proof. The decision depends on \kappa_T through the storage dynamics. A sign change in the decision under two admissible values of an unmeasured parameter means the recommendation is not determined by the available information. \square

I.2 Envelope as a declared assumption

Proposition I.2. The InSight cell qualification range (charge and discharge rated from approximately -30^\circ\mathrm{C} to +35^\circ\mathrm{C}) and the warm-box architecture establish that \kappa_T is bounded away from zero at the operational envelope, but do not pin down \kappa_T at finer temperature resolution. The declaration \kappa_T\in[\kappa_T^{\min},1] with \kappa_T^{\min} fixed at the audit is therefore the tightest defensible assumption without a cell-level characterisation curve.

Proof. The qualification range establishes positivity, not magnitude. A point value at low temperature requires a characterisation curve at the relevant temperature. Absent the curve, the band is the maximum-entropy assumption consistent with the qualification range. \square

